  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🧙‍♀️ Swamp Witch Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Witches of the bog, fen and bayou, and the hags who live where the path ends: Granny Nettle Bogwort, Maud Reedwick."
    },
    "intro": "A swamp witch's name should sound like it's been left out in the damp: a homely first name, a surname full of mud and roots, and maybe a title nobody dares drop. This **swamp witch name generator** has names for hags, bayou witches, English fen witches and gothic ones, with an epithet if you want one.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Witches of the water in folklore",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Jenny Greenteeth</strong> haunted the ponds and rivers of Lancashire, pulling children under if they went too near the edge. <strong>Peg Powler</strong> did the same on the River Tees.</li><li><strong>Will-o'-the-wisps</strong>, the ghostly lights seen over marshes, were said to lead travellers off the path and into the bog.</li><li><strong>Baba Yaga</strong>, of Slavic folklore, lives deep in the forest in a hut on chicken legs: not a swamp witch, but the model for every witch who lives where the path ends.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Hags in D&D",
        "content": "<p>In D&amp;D, the <strong>green hag</strong> lives in swamps and forests, and hags often gather in covens of three. Published hags have names that are half homely, half horrible, like the coven in <em>The Wild Beyond the Witchlight</em>.</p><p>The \"Hag\" style here follows that pattern: a title like <em>Granny</em> or <em>Auntie</em>, a herb or a hedge plant for a first name, and a surname from the bottom of the bog. <em>Granny Nettle Bogwort</em> sounds friendly until you meet her.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Four kinds of swamp witch",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Hag</strong>: herb names (Nettle, Henbane, Wormwood) and a title. Choose this for a D&amp;D hag or a fairy-tale witch.</li><li><strong>Bayou</strong>: French Creole first names from the Louisiana bayous, for Southern Gothic stories.</li><li><strong>English fen</strong>: old country names like Maud and Agnes, for the flat, misty fens of eastern England.</li><li><strong>Gothic</strong>: names from Romantic poetry and tragedy. Ophelia drowns in <em>Hamlet</em>; Lenore and Ligeia are Poe's.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What is a good name for a D&D hag?",
        "answer": "A homely title, a plant and something unpleasant: Granny Nettle Bogwort, Auntie Henbane Leechfinger. Choose the Hag style for names like these."
      },
